Note = {5th Workshop on Nutrient Cycling and Retention in Natural and
   Constructed Wetland, Borova Lada, CZECH REPUBLIC, SEP 24-27, 2003},
Abstract = {Hong Kong's rapidly growing population, especially in the North West New
   Territories, has resulted in construction and development which has
   greatly impacted on natural and traditional constructed wetland systems,
   as they are now part of new ``peri-urban complexes{''} or totally
   surrounded by urbanisation. A range of mitigation wetland systems are
   currently being developed to address community green space/amenity,
   biodiversity and water quality issues. Development includes an
   International Wetland Park covering an area of 50 hectares close to Tin
   Shui Wai and Yuen Long New Town, the West Rail project with re-creation
   of more than 12 hectares of wetlands in the Kam Tin Valley and the
   ancient Mai Po wetland system with fish pond/wetland systems
   traditionally used for aquaculture. There are considerable opportunities
   for valuable creation and management of the ecological mitigation areas,
   however, these opportunities are challenged by community, expectations,
   water quality issues and, significantly, by visions of wetland
   park/recreation areas which are intended to attract several hundred
   thousand visitors each year. A range of opportunities, constraints and
   currently utilised strategies are discussed.},
