Note = {5th International Conference on Energy, Environment, Ecosystems and
   Sustainable Development/2nd International Conference on Landscape
   Architecture, Vouliagmeni, GREECE, SEP 28-30, 2009},
Abstract = {The predicted effects of climate change on regions with fragile
   ecosystems and high dependency on common pool resources call for an
   improved and holistic approach for the management of scarce and
   depletable natural resources. The aim of this study is to identify the
   linkage between perceptions and environmental management, focusing on
   water issues in the case of Cyprus. To realise the aim, in-depth
   interviews were conducted with students of the University of Cyprus and
   locals of Panagia village. Additionally, results obtained from the
   author's participation in a policy experiment on irrigation in the above
   mentioned locations were used as a triangulation means. The results
   illustrate that environmental perceptions differ substantially between
   the urban and the rural sample, that educational level is not as a
   determinant factor in the formation of environmental perception as
   interaction with the natural environment is. A need for a more thorough
   research on environmental perceptions and their role in the decision
   making process in Cyprus was identified. Moreover, the observed lack of
   environmental awareness urges for coherent educational campaigns.},
